WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
<v Educator>Hey and welcome to this lecture on ElastiCache.</v>

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.590
So in this lecture, I'm not going to teach you

3
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.760
anything groundbreaking,

4
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.460
but we are going to see a few solution architecture,

5
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.400
and also learn the difference between Redis and Memcached,

6
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.990
because this is something you may have to choose

7
00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.910
between at the exam.

8
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.230
So the same way we have RDS

9
00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.030
to get manager international databases,

10
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.090
we get ElastiCache to get and manage Redis or Memcached.

11
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.400
And they're different technologies for caching.

12
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.680
Caches are in-memory databases

13
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.380
that have really high performance and really low latency,

14
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.200
and they help reduce load off of databases

15
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.090
for read intensive workloads.

16
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.790
So the idea is that anything that is frequently queried

17
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.350
or very expensive to compute,

18
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.840
you would cache it into an ElastiCache database,

19
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.100
so Redis or Memcached.

20
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.320
And then the database itself

21
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.390
will achieve a lot less queries.

22
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.120
They also help you make your application stateless.

23
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.520
So if you have session stores, all these kind of things,

24
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.240
ElastiCache is a great place to have this data in.

25
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.640
Now because this is a managed service,

26
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.920
AWS will take care of all the OS maintenance,

27
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.810
the patching, any optimization,

28
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.030
set up, configuration, monitoring, failure,

29
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.320
recovery, and backups.

30
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.450
You do have to set up your own settings,

31
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.390
and you do have to set up your own security groups

32
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.340
to get access to ElastiCache.

33
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.050
But overall this is a managed service.

34
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.470
So we get all the things we already know from AWS.

35
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.320
Overall using ElastiCache does involve

36
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.030
heavy application code changes.

37
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.500
This is not something that you get for free.

38
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.560
So even though you do set up a Redis or a Memcached,

39
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.940
your application code itself

40
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.790
has to be refactored to use ElastiCache.

41
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.680
So this is something you have to take into account

42
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.800
when answering a question in the exam.

43
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.980
Next, so ElastiCachee can be used at a DB Cache.

44
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.780
So the idea is that we have our application,

45
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.140
ElastiCache and for example, a database such as Amazon RDS.

46
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.450
And the application when they're refactored,

47
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.240
will query ElastiCache and if not available,

48
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.076
they will get the data from RDS and store in ElastiCache.

49
00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:52.560
That is one caging strategy.

50
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.730
So it's called lazy loading.

51
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.770
So if we get a cache hit, that's perfect,

52
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.750
we get the data back from ElastiCache.

53
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.950
Otherwise, if you have a Cache miss

54
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.110
because the data is not in ElastiCache,

55
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.090
then we're going to read it from the database,

56
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.340
and then write it to Cache.

57
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.020
This helps relieve any kind of load

58
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.120
in RDS in case there is some hot keys

59
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.400
or something that reads very, very often.

60
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.010
And you must have a cache invalidation strategy

61
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.320
to make sure that only the most current data

62
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.280
is used in there.

63
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.260
Otherwise there will be a consistency problem

64
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.200
between the state of your cache and the state of RDS.

65
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.790
And this is a big problem in notifications.

66
00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.890
This is very hard problem to solve.

67
00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.320
Okay, the second thing can use ElastiCache for is

68
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.970
to be a user session store.

69
00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.440
So say you have your application,

70
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.900
and they're on two different EC2 instances.

71
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.310
And your users, they're accessing it

72
00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.143
through a load announcer,

73
00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:38.130
but you haven't enabled session stickiness.

74
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.900
It's possible that your users are talking to

75
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.300
any of the applications.

76
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.470
Now, what the application will do is

77
00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.740
that they will write the session data back into ElastiCache,

78
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.040
such as if your users hits another EC2 instance

79
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.240
of your application,

80
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.920
then your application can retrieve

81
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.330
the session from ElastiCache.

82
00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.910
And know who the user is if it's logged in,

83
00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.560
it's state, and that kind of things.

84
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.800
And so we have this way achieved statelessness

85
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.862
in our solution architecture regardless of

86
00:03:04.862 --> 00:03:06.780
if we hit one application or another.

87
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.020
And that is a very common pattern for using ElastiCache

88
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.150
to have user session store.

89
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.180
Okay, so finally, we need to understand the difference

90
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.680
between Redis and Memcached.

91
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.530
'Cause it may be something we have to choose on at the exam.

92
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.810
So Redis is going to have a feature

93
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.000
of multi AZ with Auto-Failover.

94
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.530
So it is highly available.

95
00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.510
There is some standby active,

96
00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.420
and you can recover from a failure of an AZ.

97
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.800
So that makes it a very good choice

98
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.450
based on some situations.

99
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.880
And you can create Read Replicas to scale reads

100
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.410
and have high availability.

101
00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.420
So this looks a lot like RDS.

102
00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.420
Redis RDS nano technique maybe that works for you.

103
00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.360
Okay, are the features that it's persistent the data.

104
00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.830
So that means that if you enable

105
00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.140
the append only file feature, AOF,

106
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.530
you can get back your data after losing your cluster.

107
00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.660
So it is possible for you to have backup

108
00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.000
and restore features, and that is extremely important.

109
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.550
So this looks a lot like a real database

110
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.470
from RDS that we're used to do.

111
00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.890
So if you look at Redis, think Redis are replication.

112
00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.570
So we have two Redis instances

113
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.400
and some replication happening between them.

114
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.960
So what you need to remember out of Redis is

115
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.610
that it's highly available.

116
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:13.200
There's some replication, some backup,

117
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.670
all that kind of things.

118
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.680
Memcached is another strategy.

119
00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.200
In this case, when you have multiple nodes in Memcached,

120
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.550
this is not for replication of data,

121
00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.920
this is for partitioning and data sharding.

122
00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:24.420
It's not a persistent cache that mean

123
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.760
that if you lose the cache then you lose your data.

124
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.690
So it's really just for temporary persist transient data.

125
00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.160
You have backup and restore features

126
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.260
for the serverless version of Memcached,

127
00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.240
not the self-managed version.

128
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.610
And it's a multi-threaded architecture.

129
00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.930
So when you look at Memcached,

130
00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.300
this is maybe not something that's gonna be Multi AZ

131
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.160
in the same way Redis is.

132
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.470
The way you should look at Memcached is sharding.

133
00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.850
So that means that some data will go

134
00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.560
onto one Memcached node.

135
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.230
Some data will go onto another Memcached node.

136
00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.570
And this way your data is sharded across multiple

137
00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.070
instances of Memcached.

138
00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.800
And if you lose one node of Memcached,

139
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.570
then you lose all the data on that node,

140
00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.889
even though the other nodes may

141
00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:04.290
still may still be operational.

142
00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:05.610
Okay, so that's it.

143
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.457
Hope that makes sense for the differences between

144
00:05:07.457 --> 00:05:09.780
Redis and Memcached and ElistiCache role.

145
00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:10.740
I hope you like this lecture,

146
00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.690
and I will see you in the next lecture.

